 &lt;p&gt;Please refer to the attached file.&lt;/p&gt;
 &lt;p&gt;Since January 2026, 84 incidents have affected humanitarian workers in Central African Republic.&lt;br&gt;
In September, 14 incidents were reported, compared to six in August, an increase of 133 per cent.&lt;br&gt;
This increase may be related to the expansion of humanitarian activities in areas with the presence&lt;br&gt;
of armed groups, as well as administrative restrictions. Despite mitigations measures, humanitarian&lt;br&gt;
personnel continue to be exposed to security incidents in high-risk areas. .&lt;br&gt;
Out of 14 incidents recorded in September, the majority (nine) were criminals in nature, including six&lt;br&gt;
cases of theft and three robberies. Four incidents involved harassment and interference affecting&lt;br&gt;
humanitarian movements, while one incident involved violence during road travel.&lt;br&gt;
International NGOS were most affected organizations, with six incidents recorded, followed by&lt;br&gt;
United Nations agencies with five incidents and national NGO with three incidents.&lt;br&gt;
Since January 2026, the Vakaga prefecture has remained the most affected with 20 incidents&lt;br&gt;
recorded, followed by Bangui with 17 incidents and Haut-Mbomou with 16 incidents.&lt;/p&gt;

