&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;Staple food (wheat flour, edible oils, and pulses) prices remained &lt;strong&gt;24 percent above pre-Middle East conflict levels&lt;/strong&gt;, as of 22 October, reflecting a combination of domestic market factors and higher fuel and import costs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Wheat flour prices recorded a slight increase (1.0%) during the week, while overall remained &lt;strong&gt;31 percent above pre-conflict levels&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The cost of basic food basket for a six-member household stood at &lt;strong&gt;PKR 18,719 per month (USD 67)&lt;/strong&gt; equivalent to &lt;strong&gt;PKR 4,680 per&lt;/strong&gt; week (USD 16.6) nationwide, excluding lower cost items such as vegetables or spices.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Petrol and diesel prices remain 47% and 41%&lt;/strong&gt; above pre-conflict levels despite a slight decline in prices of petrol (-3.6%) and diesel (-1.7%) this week.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Regional energy market volatility and disruptions to key shipping routes continue to exert upward pressure on domestic inflation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Food prices remain elevated despite adequate commodity supplies and stable market availability.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

