&lt;div class="tag country"&gt;Countries: Uganda, Kenya&lt;/div&gt;
 &lt;div class="tag source"&gt;Source: International Organization for Migration&lt;/div&gt;
 &lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://reliefweb.int/sites/default/files/styles/thumbnail/public/previews/4f/f9/4ff94dba-c0d8-5cba-8f09-844bc76ff7f1.png" alt&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
 &lt;p&gt;Please refer to the attached Infographic.&lt;/p&gt;
 &lt;p&gt;Cross-border mobility of people and livestock remains a critical adaptive strategy across the East and Horn of Africa, notably in arid and semi-arid lands where pastoral communities’ transboundary movement is common. In the Intergovernmental Authority on Development (IGAD) Protocol on Transhumance it is underscored that pastoralism is essential for safeguarding and increasing production and productivity of livestock and crops in response to climate change, variability and the pressure on existing resources.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The pilot study highlights that transhumance remains a mainstay to livelihoods across the Kenya–Uganda border in the Karamoja cluster. Seasonal transhumant movements are primarily driven by the search for pasture and water, with climate change increasingly altering migration patterns and driving earlier or later than usual movements. Cross-border mobility is common across the cluster, supported by traditional and government resource-sharing agreements, yet challenged by disease outbreaks, limited veterinary services, and mounting pressure on water resources. Early warning systems rooted in traditional indicators remain vital, but tensions at waterpoints and deteriorating livestock health underscore the fragility of pastoral systems. The Transhumance Tracking Tool (TTT) emerges as a valuable innovation, offering data-driven insights to guide conflict mitigation, resource management, and climate adaptation strategies.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Key Findings:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Forty-one per cent of the herding groups moved mainly during the dry season (December–March and July–September) seeking pasture and water. Other transhumant movements were during both dry and wet (29%) seasons, followed by movements recorded only in the wet season (26%) (April–June and October–November) and no regular seasonal pattern (4%).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Attributed to climate change impact, 58 per cent of the herding groups moved in search of pasture and water either earlier or later than usual time of the year. This contributes to 59 per cent of the cross-border movements.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The majority (89%) of the herding groups who move across the borders of Kenya and Uganda in parts of the Karamoja cluster cited search for water and pasture during dry season as driver of transhumant movements.&lt;/p&gt;

